## Propagating Trace Context Through RabbitMQ

The issue is that RabbitMQ doesn't automatically carry W3C `traceparent`/`tracestate` headers. You need to manually inject context into message headers on publish and extract it on consume.

### Service A — Publisher (Inject)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using OpenTelemetry;
using OpenTelemetry.Context.Propagation;
using RabbitMQ.Client;

private static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceA.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

public void PublishOrder(Order order, IModel channel)
{
    using var activity = Source.StartActivity("OrderEvent publish", ActivityKind.Producer);

    var props = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
    props.Headers ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

    // Inject current trace context into message headers
    Propagator.Inject(
        new PropagationContext(activity!.Context, Baggage.Current),
        props.Headers,
        (headers, key, value) => headers[key] = value);

    var body = JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(order);
    channel.BasicPublish("orders-exchange", "order.created", props, body);
}
```

### Service B — Consumer (Extract)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Text;
using OpenTelemetry;
using OpenTelemetry.Context.Propagation;
using RabbitMQ.Client.Events;

private static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceB.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

private void OnMessageReceived(object? sender, BasicDeliverEventArgs ea)
{
    // Extract trace context from message headers
    var parentContext = Propagator.Extract(
        default,
        ea.BasicProperties.Headers,
        (headers, key) =>
        {
            if (headers != null && headers.TryGetValue(key, out var val))
            {
                // RabbitMQ stores header values as byte[]
                return new[] { val is byte[] bytes ? Encoding.UTF8.GetString(bytes) : val.ToString()! };
            }
            return Enumerable.Empty<string>();
        });

    Baggage.Current = parentContext.Baggage;

    // Link the consumer span to the producer's trace
    using var activity = Source.StartActivity(
        "OrderEvent consume",
        ActivityKind.Consumer,
        parentContext.ActivityContext);  // <-- this is the key link

    var order = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Order>(ea.Body.Span);
    ProcessOrder(order!);
}
```

### OTel Registration (both services)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t
        .AddSource("ServiceA.Messaging")   // or ServiceB.Messaging
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddOtlpExporter());
```

### Why this works

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| **Inject** | `Propagator.Inject` writes `traceparent` and `tracestate` into `IBasicProperties.Headers` |
| **Extract** | `Propagator.Extract` reads them back (decoding `byte[]` → `string`) |
| **Parent link** | Passing `parentContext.ActivityContext` to `StartActivity` makes the consumer span a child of the producer span — same trace ID |

The `byte[]` decode in the extractor is critical — RabbitMQ's .NET client stores string header values as `byte[]`, not `string`.
